575: real line bundle over the source elliptic neighbourhood. It is
576: trivial there because its Chern class has degree zero on every
577: component. The remaining generator acts as \(-Q\) with a strictly
578: positive (or strictly negative) real displacement, uniformly after
579: scaling. Rescaling a smooth collar coordinate in its fundamental
580: domain gives the mapping torus of \(-Q\), over the elliptic base.
581: Its seven critical points form a curve covering the suspension
582: circle seven times. A fixed suspension coordinate therefore retains
583: the entire \(I_7\) elliptic fibre. Consequently the page
584: has four \(I_7\) fibres and eight \(I_1\) fibres, with Euler number
585: \(4\cdot7+8=36\). Its elliptic completion has
586: \(\chi(\mathcal O)=3\) and \(\sigma=-24\).
587: 
588: The page line's Picard point is \(R=7G\), since the original line is
589: \(M\); the translation point \(Q=2G\) is the distinct suspension
590: period. Height multiplies by the cover degree, giving \(h(R)=14\).
591: It lies in the identity component of every \(I_7\), so all local
592: height corrections are zero. The formula
593: \(h(R)=2\chi+2R\cdot O\) gives \(R\cdot O=4\). Equivalently these
594: are the four unramified pullbacks of the already located ordinary
595: intersection at \(t=-10\). Therefore
596: \[
597:  c_1(M)^2=-2\chi-2R\cdot O=-14.
598: \]
599: Choose the page phase disjoint from the fixed sphere. The argument
600: of P3F.10 identifies the actual circle line with this Picard point on
601: the affine torsor, with any difference pulled back from the base with
602: boundary. Its boundary Chern class is zero; the relative cup-product
603: identity there makes its relative square independent of the chosen
604: boundary trivialization. The same relative tangent comparison gives
605: \(p_{\rm rel}=3\sigma=-72\). This proves the page expression four.
606: 
607: The actual return-map comparison is now supplied by the real trace
608: P3K.6--7, with the positive parameter direction fixed in P3F.23.
609: The two orientation reversals are written explicitly in P3F.25.
610: Consequently this page expression gives the signed return integer
